Enforcement Decree of the Act on Value Enhancement of Hanok and Other Architectural Assets

Article 1 (Purpose)
The purpose of this Decree is to prescribe matters delegated by the Act on Value Enhancement of Hanok and Other Architectural Assets and matters necessary for its enforcement.

Article 2 (Formulation of Master Plans for Promotion of Architectural Assets, etc.)
(1) "Minor matters prescribed by Presidential Decree" in the proviso to Article 4 (2) of the Act on Value Enhancement of Hanok and Other Architectural Assets (hereinafter referred to as the "Act") means matters falling under any of the following subparagraphs:
1. Changing the method of implementing a project to the extent that the basic direction of the master plan for promotion of architectural assets under Article 4 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as the "master plan") remains unchanged;
2. Changing the project period for each sector specified in the master plan by up to two years;
3. Correcting a calculation error, clerical error, omission, or comparable manifest error.
(2) "Matters prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 4 (3) 8 of the Act means matters concerning the establishment and management of data on architectural assets.
(3) Upon finalizing or amending the master plan, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall, without delay, publicly announce its main contents in the Official Gazette and send it to the heads of relevant central administrative agencies and the Special Metropolitan City Mayor, Metropolitan City Mayors, Special Self-Governing City Mayor, Do Governors, or Special Self-Governing Province Governors (hereinafter referred to as "Mayors/Do Governors").
(4) A Mayor/Do Governor shall, without delay, send the master plan received under paragraph (3) to the heads of Sis/Guns/Gus under his or her jurisdiction (in the case of the head of a Gu, referring to the head of an autonomous Gu; hereinafter the same shall apply).

Article 3 (Formulation of Implementation Plans for Promotion of Architectural Assets, etc.)
(1) An implementation plan for promotion of architectural assets under Article 5 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as an "implementation plan") shall include the following matters: <Amended on February 9, 2018>
1. Achievements in carrying out the preceding implementation plan;
2. Directions for carrying out projects each year;
3. Major annual project plans and detailed plans for each project;
4. Other matters necessary for the conservation, utilization, and creation of architectural assets.
(2) Before submitting a proposed implementation plan for deliberation by the Metropolitan Building Committee under Article 5 (2) of the Act, a Mayor/Do Governor shall hear the opinions of the heads of Sis/Guns/Gus under his or her jurisdiction. <Amended on February 9, 2018>
(3) A Mayor/Do Governor shall submit an implementation plan to the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport within 60 days after formulating it. <Amended on February 9, 2018>
(4) A Mayor/Do Governor shall publicly announce an implementation plan through the official bulletin and website of the relevant Special Metropolitan City, Metropolitan City, Special Self-Governing City, Do, or Special Self-Governing Province (hereinafter referred to as a "City/Do") and send it to the heads of Sis/Guns/Gus under his or her jurisdiction. <Amended on February 9, 2018>
(5) A Mayor/Do Governor who intends to amend an established implementation plan shall comply with Article 5 (2) of the Act and paragraph (2) of this Article: Provided, That those procedures may be omitted when changing minor matters prescribed by ordinance of the relevant City/Do.
(6) A Mayor/Do Governor shall, without delay, send an implementation plan amended under paragraph (5) to the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport and the heads of Sis/Guns/Gus under his or her jurisdiction, and publicly announce it through the official bulletin and website of the relevant City/Do.

Article 4 (Basic Surveys of Architectural Assets)
(1) Surveys of architectural assets under Article 6 (1) of the Act shall include the following matters:
1. The following basic information on the architectural assets:
(a) Information such as the year of construction, size, and owner;
(b) Use, structure, and materials;
(c) Status of alterations and repairs;
(d) State of preservation and management;
(e) Designer and contractor;
(f) Other necessary matters;
2. The following matters for assessing the value of the architectural assets:
(a) Major historical developments;
(b) Characteristics and principal values;
(c) Principal portions to be conserved;
(d) Value as architectural assets;
(e) Matters necessary for conservation, utilization, and management;
3. Photographic materials on the architectural assets.
(2) Where necessary, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may determine the methods and standards for surveys of architectural assets under Article 6 (1) of the Act and notify Mayors/Do Governors thereof.

Article 5 (Establishment and Operation of Architectural Assets Information Systems)
(1) An architectural assets information system under Article 7 (1) of the Act shall include the following:
1. Basic information, including the name, location, and owner of architectural assets;
2. Materials on the conservation, management, and utilization of architectural assets;
3. Materials on the survey, identification, and research of architectural assets;
4. Visual materials on architectural assets, including photographs, drawings, and videos;
5. Other materials to assist understanding of the relevant architectural assets.
(2) A Mayor/Do Governor may compile the materials specified in the subparagraphs of paragraph (1) in the form of electronic information, publications, or other formats and provide them through websites or other means so that the public can readily use their contents.

Article 6 (Support for Businesses Related to Maintenance and Repair of Architectural Assets)
(1) Businesses eligible for support under Article 9 (1) of the Act are as follows: <Amended on September 14, 2021; May 7, 2024>
1. Persons who have filed a report on the opening of an architect's office under Article 23 of the Architects Act;
2. Persons who have registered a construction business under Article 9 of the Framework Act on the Construction Industry;
3. Persons who have registered a construction engineering business under Article 26 of the Construction Technology Promotion Act;
4. Persons who have registered a national heritage repair business, a national heritage measured survey and design business, or a national heritage supervision business under Article 14 of the Act on Repair of National Heritage, etc.;
5. Other persons conducting businesses related to the maintenance and repair of architectural assets that are prescribed and publicly notified by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport or prescribed by ordinance of the relevant local government.
(2) The head of a central administrative agency or the head of a local government who intends to provide support under Article 9 (1) of the Act shall determine the details and procedures of the support and other relevant matters and publicly announce them in the Official Gazette, an official bulletin, and on a website for a period of at least 30 days.

Article 7 (Registration of Outstanding Architectural Assets)
(1) Architectural assets to be registered as outstanding architectural assets under Article 10 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as "outstanding architectural assets") shall meet all of the following standards:
1. They shall possess artistic, historical, landscape, or sociocultural value as specified in Annex 1;
2. There shall be a need to systematically maintain and manage the value specified in subparagraph 1, or a risk that such value may be lost or impaired if the assets are left unattended.
(2) An owner of architectural assets who intends to apply for registration of outstanding architectural assets under Article 10 (1) of the Act shall submit an application to the Mayor/Do Governor, accompanied by the following materials:
1. Documentary and visual materials on the architectural assets;
2. Explanatory materials concerning the standards specified in the subparagraphs of paragraph (1).
(3) Upon receipt of an application under paragraph (2), a Mayor/Do Governor shall conduct a survey under Article 6 of the Act: Provided, That existing survey results, if any, may be used.
(4) A Mayor/Do Governor shall decide whether to register the outstanding architectural assets and notify the applicant within 60 days from the date of receipt of an application under paragraph (2): Provided, That, in unavoidable circumstances, the notification period may be extended by up to 30 days.
(5) When registering outstanding architectural assets, a Mayor/Do Governor shall determine the name of the architectural assets and may prescribe matters necessary for their conservation. In such cases, he or she shall consult in advance with the owner of the architectural assets concerning their name.
(6) Where a Mayor/Do Governor registers outstanding architectural assets, he or she shall issue a registration certificate to the applicant.

Article 8 (Register of Outstanding Architectural Assets, etc.)
(1) The register of outstanding architectural assets under Article 12(3) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as the "register of outstanding architectural assets") shall contain the following matters: <Amended on February 9, 2018>
1. The name, location, and owner of the relevant architectural asset;
2. The use, structure, size, and principal materials of the relevant architectural asset;
3. The year of construction, designer, and contractor of the relevant architectural asset;
4. The date of registration as an outstanding architectural asset and the date of cancellation of registration;
5. The history of the relevant architectural asset;
6. The principal values of the relevant architectural asset and other matters requiring preservation;
7. Matters concerning support under Article 12 of the Act;
8. Matters concerning permission for, and reports on, extension, rebuilding, demolition, etc. under Article 13 of the Act;
9. Matters concerning the application of special provisions under Article 14 of the Act.
(2) Where the relevant outstanding architectural asset is a building as defined in Article 2(1)2 of the Building Act, the Mayor/Do Governor shall record the fact of its registration as an outstanding architectural asset and the matters referred to in paragraph (1)6 and 8 in the building register under Article 38 of the Building Act (hereinafter referred to as the "building register"). Provided, That where there is a separate building permit authority having jurisdiction over the relevant building (meaning the permitting authority under Article 11 of the Building Act; hereinafter the same shall apply), the Mayor/Do Governor shall notify that building permit authority so that it records those matters in the building register.

Article 9 (Reports and Permission for Extension, Rebuilding, Demolition, etc. of Outstanding Architectural Assets)
(1) "Matters prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 13(1)3 of the Act means acts that alter the matters referred to in Article 8(1)6.
(2) A person who intends to file a report under Article 13(1) of the Act or obtain permission under paragraph (3) of that Article shall, where the relevant outstanding architectural asset is a building and there is a separate building permit authority, file the report or apply for permission to the Mayor/Do Governor through that building permit authority.
(3) Where a Mayor/Do Governor receives an application for permission under the former part of Article 13(3) of the Act, the Mayor/Do Governor shall, unless there is a special reason otherwise, grant or deny permission within 60 days and notify the applicant in writing of the result (including the grounds therefor in the case of a denial of permission). <Added on February 9, 2018>
(4) Where a Mayor/Do Governor receives a report under Article 13(1) of the Act, provides guidance, advice, or recommendations, etc. under paragraph (2) of that Article, or grants permission under paragraph (3) of that Article, and the relevant outstanding architectural asset is a building, the Mayor/Do Governor shall notify the building permit authority having jurisdiction over the relevant building of that fact without delay. <Amended on February 9, 2018>

Article 10 (Application of Special Provisions of Relevant Statutes and Regulations to Outstanding Architectural Assets)
When applying special provisions under Article 14(1) of the Act, all of the following criteria shall be met:
1. The work for which the application of special provisions is sought shall constitute extension, rebuilding, reconstruction, or substantial repair under Article 2 of the Enforcement Decree of the Building Act;
2. The principal values of the relevant outstanding architectural asset shall be maintained even if special provisions are applied;
3. The application of special provisions shall not cause excessive disadvantage to surrounding building sites and buildings;
4. The application of special provisions shall not unduly impair the aesthetic appearance or environment of the city.

Article 11 (Cancellation of Registration of Outstanding Architectural Assets)
(1) A person who intends to have the registration of an outstanding architectural asset cancelled under Article 15(1) of the Act shall, where the relevant outstanding architectural asset is a building and there is a separate building permit authority, submit a request to the Mayor/Do Governor through that building permit authority.
(2) The owner of an outstanding architectural asset falling under any subparagraph of Article 13(3) of the Act may not request cancellation of its registration as an outstanding architectural asset under Article 15(1) of the Act before the grace period for the relevant category under the following subparagraphs has elapsed, except where the value of the outstanding architectural asset has been lost due to unavoidable circumstances. In such cases, where two or more of the criteria in the following subparagraphs apply, the latest date shall be used as the basis:
1. Where a tax reduction or exemption has been granted under Article 12(1) of the Act: One year from the date on which the tax reduction or exemption was last granted;
2. Where a subsidy or loan has been provided under Article 12(2) of the Act: Five years from the date on which it was provided. Provided, That this shall not apply where the subsidy or loan provided has been returned or repaid in full;
3. Where approval for use has been obtained or a completion inspection has been conducted through the application of special provisions of relevant statutes and regulations under Article 14 of the Act: Five years from the date on which approval for use was obtained or the completion inspection was conducted. Provided, That this shall not apply where the portion to which special provisions were applied has been restored to its original state with permission under Article 13(3) of the Act.
(3) Where a Mayor/Do Governor determines that registration needs to be cancelled under Article 15(2) of the Act or receives a request for cancellation of registration, the Mayor/Do Governor shall review the relevant documents and conduct an on-site investigation, etc.; where the relevant outstanding architectural asset is a building and there is a separate building permit authority, the Mayor/Do Governor shall consult in advance with that building permit authority before deliberation by the Building Committee (meaning the Building Committee under Article 4 of the Building Act; hereinafter the same shall apply) under the latter part of Article 15(2) of the Act.
(4) The owner of an outstanding architectural asset whose registration has been cancelled under Article 15(2) of the Act shall return the registration mark for outstanding architectural assets under Article 10(3) of the Act and the certificate of registration of outstanding architectural assets under Article 7(6) to the Mayor/Do Governor.
(5) Where a Mayor/Do Governor cancels the registration of an outstanding architectural asset under Article 15(2) of the Act, the Mayor/Do Governor shall record that fact in both the register of outstanding architectural assets and the building register. Provided, That where the relevant outstanding architectural asset is a building and there is a separate building permit authority, the Mayor/Do Governor shall notify that building permit authority so that it records that fact in the building register.
(6) Where a Mayor/Do Governor cancels the registration of an outstanding architectural asset under Article 15(2) of the Act, and the relevant architectural asset has been subject to special provisions under Article 14 of the Act but has not yet obtained approval for use or undergone a completion inspection under those special provisions, the Mayor/Do Governor shall cancel the application of those special provisions. Provided, That where the person who applied the special provisions is the head of a Si/Gun/Gu, the Mayor/Do Governor shall notify the relevant head of a Si/Gun/Gu so that the application of the special provisions is cancelled.

Article 12 (Procedures for Designation, Modification, and Cancellation of Architectural Asset Promotion Districts)
(1) A Mayor/Do Governor who intends to designate an architectural asset promotion district pursuant to Article 17 (1) of the Act shall, based on the results of the survey under Article 6 of the Act, prepare a plan for the designation of an architectural asset promotion district (hereinafter referred to as a "designation plan") that includes the matters specified in the subparagraphs of Article 20 (1) of the Act, and consult with the head of the Si/Gun having jurisdiction over the relevant area.
(2) A Mayor/Do Governor shall publicly announce the details of the designation plan under paragraph (1) in the official gazette and on the website of the relevant City/Do for at least 14 days and hear the opinions of residents of the relevant area.
(3) Where a Mayor/Do Governor undergoes deliberation conducted jointly by the Building Committee and the local urban planning committee under Article 113 of the National Land Planning and Utilization Act pursuant to Articles 17 (1) and 18 (1) of the Act, the criteria specified in the subparagraphs of Article 110-7 (2) of the Enforcement Decree of the Building Act shall apply mutatis mutandis to the criteria for the composition of the relevant committee (hereinafter referred to as the "Joint Deliberation Committee"). In such cases, "Integrated Deliberation Committee" shall be construed as "Joint Deliberation Committee," and "Mayor/Do Governor or head of a Si/Gun/Gu" as "head of the relevant local government." <Added on February 9, 2018>
(4) A Mayor/Do Governor who intends to modify or cancel an architectural asset promotion district designated pursuant to Article 17 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as an "architectural asset promotion district") pursuant to Article 18 of the Act shall follow the procedures under paragraphs (1) and (2). <Amended on February 9, 2018>

Article 13 (Contents of Management Plans for Architectural Asset Promotion Districts)
(1) "Matters prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 19 (1) 11 of the Act means the following matters: <Amended on February 9, 2018>
1. Plans for infrastructure, such as water supply and sewerage systems and roads;
2. Disaster prevention plans;
3. Plans for facilities to be installed in underground or overhead spaces;
4. Plans for the utilization and management of buildings not entered in the building register.
(2) "Those prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 19 (4) 2 of the Act means karaoke bar business and entertainment bar business under Article 21 (8) (c) and (d) of the Enforcement Decree of the Food Sanitation Act. <Added on February 9, 2018>
(3) Where the head of a local government prescribes by ordinance the businesses or installation of facilities to be prohibited or restricted in an architectural asset promotion district pursuant to Article 19 (4) 3 of the Act, the head shall take into consideration the cultural value and characteristics of the architectural asset promotion district and the purpose of its designation. <Added on February 9, 2018>

Article 14 (Procedures for Formulation of Management Plans for Architectural Asset Promotion Districts)
(1) The Special Metropolitan City Mayor, a Metropolitan City Mayor, a Special Self-Governing City Mayor, a Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun having jurisdiction over an architectural asset promotion district who intends to formulate a management plan for the architectural asset promotion district under Article 19 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as a "management plan") shall publicly announce its main details in at least two daily newspapers and on the website of the relevant authority so that residents may inspect them for at least 14 days.
(2) Any person who has an opinion on a management plan may submit the opinion to the Special Metropolitan City Mayor, a Metropolitan City Mayor, a Special Self-Governing City Mayor, a Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun during the inspection period.
(3) The Special Metropolitan City Mayor, a Metropolitan City Mayor, a Special Self-Governing City Mayor, a Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun who receives an opinion submitted pursuant to paragraph (2) shall review the opinion and notify the person who submitted it whether it will be incorporated.
(4) Where the Special Metropolitan City Mayor, a Metropolitan City Mayor, a Special Self-Governing City Mayor, a Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun intends to incorporate an opinion submitted pursuant to paragraph (2) into a management plan, and its contents constitute an important matter prescribed by ordinance of the relevant local government, the procedures under paragraphs (1) through (3) shall be followed again.
(5) Where there is an administrative agency concerned with the contents of a management plan, the Special Metropolitan City Mayor, a Metropolitan City Mayor, a Special Self-Governing City Mayor, a Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun shall consult with the head of the relevant administrative agency in advance.
(6) The Special Metropolitan City Mayor, a Metropolitan City Mayor, a Special Self-Governing City Mayor, a Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun shall undergo deliberation by the Joint Deliberation Committee to finalize a management plan. <Amended on February 9, 2018>
(7) When the Special Metropolitan City Mayor, a Metropolitan City Mayor, a Special Self-Governing City Mayor, a Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun has finalized a management plan, the Mayor, Governor, or head shall publicly announce its main details in the official gazette and on the website of the relevant local government and make them available for public inspection.
(8) Paragraphs (1) through (7) shall not apply where a management plan is formulated pursuant to Article 19 (3) of the Act in accordance with the procedures for formulating an urban/Gun management plan under the National Land Planning and Utilization Act.

Article 15 (Proposals for Designation of Architectural Asset Promotion Districts and Drafting of Management Plans)
(1) A person who intends to propose the designation of an architectural asset promotion district and the drafting of a management plan pursuant to Article 20 (1) of the Act shall obtain the consent of landowners who own at least two-thirds of the land area of the relevant district (excluding the area of State-owned and public land).
(2) A Mayor/Do Governor or head of a Si/Gun who receives a proposal for the designation of an architectural asset promotion district and the drafting of a management plan pursuant to Article 20 of the Act shall notify the proposer whether the proposal will be incorporated within 30 days from the date of the proposal: Provided, That in unavoidable circumstances, the notification period may be extended only once by up to 30 days.

Article 16 (Special Cases concerning Application of Relevant Statutes and Regulations in Architectural Asset Promotion Districts)
(1) "Scope prescribed by Presidential Decree" in the portion of Article 21 (1) of the Act other than its subparagraphs means the following scope: <Amended on February 9, 2018>
1. Provisions on relaxation in district unit planning zones under Article 52 (3) of the National Land Planning and Utilization Act: The scope prescribed in Articles 45, 46, 47, and 65 of the Enforcement Decree of that Act;
2. Articles 46, 47, 58, and 59 of the Building Act: The scope prescribed in a district unit plan under Article 49 of the National Land Planning and Utilization Act.
(2) To apply the special cases under Article 21 of the Act within the scope specified in each subparagraph of paragraph (1), all of the following criteria shall be met:
1. The public interest shall not be harmed;
2. The aesthetic appearance of the city shall be improved or the continuity of the streetscape shall be preserved.
(3) "Scope prescribed by Presidential Decree" in the former part of Article 21 (2) of the Act means 90 percent or less. <Added on February 9, 2018>

Article 17 (Support for Hanok Construction and Creation of Hanok Villages)
(1) Eligibility for support under Article 24 (1) of the Act shall be prescribed by ordinance of the relevant local government; a Hanok village shall have at least 10 organically interconnected Hanok houses within a single area.
(2) The support under Article 24 (1) of the Act shall consist of the following:
1. Technical advice and supervision through institutions, etc. designated by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport or the head of a local government;
2. Subsidies and loans for project expenses within the scope prescribed by ordinance of the relevant local government;
3. Other matters prescribed by ordinance of the relevant local government.

Article 18 (Applications for and Decisions on Support for Hanok Construction and Creation of Hanok Villages)
"Documents prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as plans for Hanok construction and the creation of Hanok villages" in Article 25 (1) of the Act means the documents classified as follows:
1. Where a person intends to apply for support for Hanok construction, the documents specified in the following items:
(a) An application for support;
(b) A Hanok construction plan;
(c) Documents proving the extent of the building site and the right to own or use that site;
(d) A location map and photographs of current conditions;
(e) Design documents, such as site plans, floor plans, elevations, and sections;
(f) A plan for expenditure of project expenses (only where financial support is requested);
(g) A document stating the need for support;
2. Where a person intends to apply for support for the creation of a Hanok village, the documents specified in the following items:
(a) An application for support;
(b) A plan for the creation of a Hanok village;
(c) Documents proving the right to own or use the site on which the Hanok village is to be created;
(d) A location map and site plan of the area in which the Hanok village is to be created;
(e) A plan for expenditure of project expenses (only where financial support is requested);
(f) A document stating the need for support.

Article 19 (Special Cases concerning Relevant Statutes and Regulations for Hanok)
(1) The criteria for applying the special cases concerning the Building Act, the Green Buildings Construction Support Act, and the Civil Act under Article 26 of the Act shall be as specified in Annex 2. <Amended on February 9, 2018>
(2) Where it is unclear whether a building for which the application of the special cases under paragraph (1) is sought constitutes a Hanok, the building permit authority may submit the matter to the relevant Building Committee for deliberation.

Article 20 (National Hanok Center)
(1) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport designates the National Hanok Center pursuant to Article 28 (1) of the Act, the Minister shall publicly announce the designation in the Official Gazette and on the website.
(2) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport may provide support for the expenses necessary for the operation of the National Hanok Center designated pursuant to Article 28 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as the "National Hanok Center").
(3) Where the National Hanok Center receives support for expenses pursuant to paragraph (2), it shall submit a report on the settlement of accounts for the use of the expenses in the preceding year to the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport by January 31 each year.
(4) "Matters prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 28 (2) 5 of the Act means the following matters:
1. Research on standards relating to Hanok, Hanok villages, etc.;
2. Identification and research of outstanding examples relating to Hanok;
3. Functions relating to Hanok entrusted by the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport or the head of a local government.

Article 21 (Designation of Institutions for Training Hanok Industry Professionals, etc.)
(1) Where the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport or a Mayor/Do Governor intends to designate an institution for training Hanok industry professionals pursuant to Article 29 (2) of the Act, the Minister or Mayor/Do Governor shall invite applications by publicly announcing the following matters in the Official Gazette or the official bulletin of the relevant City/Do:
1. The purpose of designation;
2. The period of designation;
3. Details of financial support, etc. (only where the details of support under Article 29 (4) of the Act have been determined in advance);
4. Other matters necessary for the invitation of applications.
(2) A person who intends to respond to an invitation of applications under paragraph (1) shall submit an application to the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport or the Mayor/Do Governor, accompanied by a project plan that includes the following:
1. The track record in training professionals;
2. A plan for securing instructors, etc.;
3. A plan for operating curricula and educational content;
4. A plan for financing operating expenses and managing finances.
(3) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport or a Mayor/Do Governor shall issue a certificate of designation to a person designated as an institution for training Hanok industry professionals pursuant to Article 29 (2) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as an "institution for training Hanok industry professionals").
(4) An institution for training Hanok industry professionals shall submit a report on the results of its operations to the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport or the Mayor/Do Governor by December 31 each year.
(5) Where a local government provides support pursuant to Article 29 (4) of the Act for the expenses necessary for training professionals to an institution for training Hanok industry professionals designated pursuant to paragraph (2) of that Article, matters necessary for the scope, procedures, and methods of such support shall be prescribed by ordinance of the relevant local government. <Added on March 25, 2025>

Article 22 (Certification of Excellent Institutions for Training Hanok Professionals)
(1) A person who intends to obtain certification as an excellent institution for training personnel under Article 29 (3) of the Act shall submit an application to the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, accompanied by the following documents:
1. An explanatory statement and summary of the personnel training program for which certification is sought;
2. Documents concerning the qualifications and staffing status of specialist instructors;
3. Documents concerning the current holdings of educational facilities and equipment;
4. A plan for financing the operating expenses of the curricula;
5. A track record in research, education, and projects relating to Hanok design and construction;
6. Documents evidencing ownership of or the right to use training and practical training sites (only where this cannot be verified by a certificate of registered matters concerning a building under the Registration of Real Estate Act; documents evidencing a right of use shall specify the scope and duration of such right).
(2) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall issue a certificate to a person certified pursuant to Article 29 (3) of the Act.
(3) Certification under Article 29 (3) of the Act shall be valid for two years from the date of certification, and a person who intends to extend the period of validity shall apply for an extension during the period from six months to one month before the expiration of the period of validity.

Article 23 (Acceptance of Donated Money or Goods, etc.)
(1) Where a Mayor/Do Governor or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu receives donated money or goods under Article 36 (2) 4 of the Act (hereinafter referred to as "donated money or goods"), the Mayor/Do Governor or head of a Si/Gun/Gu shall issue a receipt to the donor: Provided, That this shall not apply where the donor cannot be identified for reasons such as an anonymous donation. <Amended on February 9, 2018>
(2) Where a donor under paragraph (1) specifies the purpose for which donated money or goods are to be used, a Mayor/Do Governor or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu shall use them only for that purpose: Provided, That where there are special reasons that make it difficult to use them for the specified purpose, they may be used for another purpose with the donor's consent. <Amended on February 9, 2018>
(3) A Mayor/Do Governor or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu shall process the receipts and disbursements of donated money or goods through an account dedicated to donations. <Amended on February 9, 2018>
(4) A Mayor/Do Governor or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu shall maintain books, documents, etc. showing the status of acceptance of donated money or goods and details of their use, and shall make them available for inspection by donors. <Amended on February 9, 2018>

Article 24 (Entrustment of Functions)
(1) The Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport shall entrust the following functions to the National Hanok Center pursuant to Article 37 (2) of the Act:
1. Receipt and evaluation of applications for designation as an institution for training Hanok industry professionals under Article 29 (2) of the Act;
2. Receipt and evaluation of applications for certification as an excellent institution for training Hanok professionals under Article 29 (3) of the Act;
3. Education and publicity under Article 33 of the Act;
4. Functions concerning the identification of and awards for outstanding examples under Article 35 of the Act.
(2) A Mayor/Do Governor may, pursuant to Article 37 (2) of the Act, designate a public institution and entrust it with functions concerning the establishment and operation of the architectural assets information system under Article 7 of the Act, and may entrust the National Hanok Center with the receipt and evaluation of applications for designation as an institution for training Hanok industry professionals under Article 29 (2) of the Act.
(3) The head of a local government may entrust the following functions to the National Hanok Center pursuant to Article 37 (2) of the Act:
1. Education and publicity under Article 33 of the Act;
2. Functions concerning the identification of and awards for outstanding examples under Article 35 of the Act.
(4) Where a Mayor/Do Governor or the head of a local government entrusts functions pursuant to paragraphs (2) and (3), the Mayor/Do Governor or head of the local government shall publicly announce the person entrusted with the functions and the details of the entrusted functions in the Official Gazette or the official bulletin of the relevant local government.

Article 25 (Criteria for Imposing Administrative Fines)
The criteria for imposing administrative fines under Article 42 of the Act shall be as specified in Annex 3.

